package com.hpcai270

// Days 118–134: storage internals, protocols and enterprise-to-HPC translation.
val hpcDaysPart13 = listOf(
Day(118,"Block storage internals","I/O path, caching and queues","Block storage presents sectors/blocks to a host. Understand application → filesystem → block layer → multipath/device mapper → transport → array/controller → media.","PATH\n\nApplication → filesystem → page cache/direct I/O → block layer → queue → driver → HBA/NIC → fabric → target → controller → media.","TOOLS\n\niostat, blktrace/bpftrace concepts, lsblk, multipath -ll, lsscsi, udevadm, /sys/block. Distinguish logical device state from physical media state.","PERFORMANCE\n\nQueue depth, request size, read/write mix, cache hit rate, controller CPU and backend media latency all affect observed latency.","LAB\n\nMap one mounted filesystem to its block device and then to the underlying path(s). Document every layer and failure domain.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can an array report low latency while the application sees high latency? Include host queueing, multipath, filesystem, network and application wait time.","GOAL\n\nNever treat a LUN as a magic disk; understand the complete I/O path."),
Day(119,"Multipath I/O","Redundant paths without duplicate disks","Multipath provides multiple host paths to the same storage device. The key is recognizing one logical device behind several physical paths.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nHost HBA/NIC A + HBA/NIC B → fabric → storage target ports → same LUN. Multipath presents one device while managing paths.","OPERATIONS\n\nCheck path states, policies, failover behavior and device identity. A path DOWN event is not necessarily a storage outage.","FAILURE\n\nIf one fabric fails, I/O should continue through surviving paths. Validate that path failures are detected quickly and do not create asymmetric performance.","LAB\n\nStudy multipath -ll output and draw active/standby paths. For every path identify host adapter, fabric, target port and fault domain.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain active-active versus active-passive behavior and why path count is not the same as usable bandwidth.","GOAL\n\nReason about redundancy at the path level."),
Day(120,"NFS internals","Client/server file access","NFS is a network filesystem, but senior troubleshooting requires understanding RPC, mounts, caching, locking, retransmissions, network latency and server-side filesystem behavior.","PATH\n\nProcess → VFS → NFS client → RPC/TCP → network → NFS server → local filesystem/storage. Mount options change semantics and performance.","TOOLS\n\nfindmnt, nfsstat, mount, /proc/mounts, ss, rpcinfo where applicable, server logs and packet capture when needed.","PERFORMANCE\n\nSmall synchronous operations are latency-sensitive. Large sequential transfers are bandwidth-sensitive. Metadata-heavy workloads can stress a completely different subsystem.","LAB\n\nMount a test NFS export with two option sets. Measure metadata and sequential performance and explain the trade-offs.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy might NFS perform well for user directories but poorly for massive distributed training? Compare metadata, concurrency, protocol overhead and parallelism.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand where NFS fits in an AI cluster instead of calling every shared filesystem ‘NFS’."),
Day(121,"NFS troubleshooting","From stale mount to server saturation","NFS incidents require separating client, network, protocol and server problems.","CHECKLIST\n\nMount state → DNS/name resolution → network path → TCP sessions → client counters → server counters → server filesystem → backend storage.","SYMPTOMS\n\nHang, stale file handle, permission denial, slow metadata, intermittent I/O, connection reset. Each symptom suggests different evidence.","TOOLS\n\nnfsstat -c/-s, ss, mount options, server CPU/memory/network/storage metrics, logs and timestamps.","SAFE RESPONSE\n\nDo not immediately force-unmount production clients. Determine whether the server or network is unhealthy and understand application impact first.","LAB\n\nCreate a troubleshooting matrix mapping five NFS symptoms to evidence and safe actions.","INTERVIEW\n\nA cluster reports ‘NFS is slow.’ What exact metrics do you request before accepting the statement?", "GOAL\n\nTurn vague performance complaints into measurable hypotheses."),
Day(122,"S3 internals","Object semantics and distributed access","S3 is object storage accessed through an API. Objects have keys, metadata and content; there is no traditional POSIX inode/directory semantics underneath the API abstraction.","MODEL\n\nClient → HTTP(S) → load balancer/API → authentication → object metadata/index → storage nodes/media → replication/EC.","AI USE\n\nDatasets, model artifacts, checkpoints, logs and archives can live in object storage. Training frameworks may stage data into POSIX or local cache for performance.","PERFORMANCE\n\nRequest concurrency, object size, prefix/key distribution, network bandwidth and backend protection influence throughput. One giant object and millions of tiny objects are different workloads.","LAB\n\nUse an S3-compatible endpoint in a lab. Upload, download, list, inspect metadata and calculate effective throughput for different object sizes.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is object storage not a drop-in replacement for a POSIX filesystem? Discuss rename, locking, directory semantics, latency and application expectations.","GOAL\n\nChoose object storage because its semantics fit the workload, not because ‘S3 is modern’."),
Day(123,"Object storage protection","Replication, erasure coding and durability","Object platforms distribute data across nodes and failure domains. Protection policy trades usable capacity, write cost, rebuild behavior and durability.","OPTIONS\n\nReplication stores multiple copies. Erasure coding stores data plus parity fragments. The right choice depends on workload, failure model and access pattern.","MATH\n\nFor k data + m parity fragments, raw overhead is (k+m)/k. This is only the beginning: metadata, reserved capacity and operational headroom also matter.","OPERATIONS\n\nMonitor degraded objects, repair queues, node failures and capacity thresholds. A durable system can still become unavailable if it exhausts recovery resources.","LAB\n\nCalculate usable capacity and fault tolerance for several k+m schemes. Then compare small-object behavior conceptually.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can erasure coding reduce capacity cost but increase write/rebuild complexity? Explain without claiming it is universally better.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand protection as an engineering trade-off."),
Day(124,"RAID and rebuild behavior","What happens after a disk dies","RAID is a local protection mechanism, not a complete backup or disaster-recovery strategy. Rebuild traffic can compete with production I/O.","LEVELS\n\nKnow RAID 0/1/5/6/10 concepts, usable capacity, failure tolerance and write penalties. Also understand why modern distributed systems may use replication/EC instead.","REBUILD\n\nFailure → degraded state → rebuild reads surviving data → reconstructs missing data → writes replacement. Workload latency can increase during recovery.","DESIGN\n\nConsider disk size, rebuild duration, failure correlation, hot spares, controller limits and fault domains.","LAB\n\nModel a RAID6 rebuild on a large drive. Estimate why rebuild duration matters to risk even when the array is technically protected.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why ‘RAID is redundant so backup is unnecessary’ is wrong.","GOAL\n\nSeparate availability, durability, backup and disaster recovery."),
Day(125,"Snapshots and clones","Fast copies with hidden cost","Snapshots are metadata and copy-on-write mechanisms in many storage systems. They are useful for recovery, testing and workflows but consume resources as data diverges.","MODEL\n\nOriginal block + snapshot reference → overwrite triggers preservation of old data according to implementation. Snapshot space and metadata grow with changed data.","AI USE\n\nSnapshots can create reproducible datasets or training checkpoints, but large concurrent snapshot/clone operations can create metadata and backend pressure.","OPERATIONS\n\nMonitor snapshot count, reserved space, changed blocks and deletion behavior. Deleting snapshots may itself generate heavy work.","LAB\n\nCreate a snapshot, modify data, measure changed space, restore selected data and document what the snapshot does not protect against.","INTERVIEW\n\nSnapshot vs backup vs clone vs replication: explain the purpose and failure domain of each.","GOAL\n\nUse snapshots deliberately rather than treating them as free backups."),
Day(126,"Storage replication","RPO, RTO and consistency","Replication copies data to another system or site. Senior engineers reason from recovery objectives, not from product features.","TERMS\n\nRPO = acceptable data loss. RTO = acceptable recovery time. Synchronous replication minimizes data loss but has distance/latency constraints; asynchronous replication accepts lag.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nPrimary → replication channel → secondary. Consider bandwidth, change rate, journal/log capacity, failover orchestration and application consistency.","FAILURE\n\nA replicated corruption can be replicated too. Recovery design must include clean recovery points and operational controls.","LAB\n\nDesign synchronous and asynchronous examples for 10 ms versus 100 ms inter-site latency. Explain the trade-offs.","INTERVIEW\n\nGiven RPO 15 minutes and RTO 1 hour, design a storage recovery strategy and explain what must be tested.","GOAL\n\nTranslate business recovery requirements into technical mechanisms."),
Day(127,"Backup architecture","Protection beyond primary storage","Backup is an independent recovery capability. A strong design separates production failure, operational mistake, ransomware/corruption and site disaster scenarios.","LAYERS\n\nPrimary data → backup target → immutable/isolated copy → offsite/cloud/archive. Recovery paths should be documented and tested.","HPC/AI\n\nNot every training dataset needs identical backup policy. Reconstructible datasets may be cheaper to regenerate; unique checkpoints, model artifacts and business data may need stronger protection.","METRICS\n\nBackup throughput, change rate, window, repository capacity, retention, immutability and restore throughput.","LAB\n\nDesign protection for a 100 TB dataset and 20 TB/day change rate. Separate operational restore from disaster recovery.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat would you protect first in an AI environment: raw datasets, checkpoints, source code, metadata or models? Answer by business value and reproducibility.","GOAL\n\nTreat recovery as a measurable service."),
Day(128,"Storage security","Identity, isolation and encryption","Storage security spans authentication, authorization, network isolation, encryption, key management, auditing and secure administration.","MODEL\n\nUser/service identity → authorization policy → data path → encryption → audit. Security must be designed without destroying performance or operability.","HPC\n\nShared clusters require controlled access to datasets and scratch. Service accounts, POSIX permissions, ACLs, project groups and network segmentation must align.","OPERATIONS\n\nLeast privilege, separate management networks, secure APIs, certificate lifecycle, key rotation and logging matter as much as firewall rules.","LAB\n\nDesign access for three research groups sharing one filesystem. Define identities, groups, ACLs, quotas and audit requirements.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why storage security is not just ‘encrypt the disks’. Include control plane, data plane and administrative access.","GOAL\n\nMake security a property of the architecture."),
Day(129,"Quotas and multi-tenancy","Preventing one team from consuming the cluster","Shared HPC storage needs governance. Quotas can limit users, groups, projects or files and protect shared capacity.","MODEL\n\nIdentity → project/group → quota policy → filesystem/object namespace → alert/enforcement.","PERFORMANCE\n\nQuota accounting itself has metadata implications. The design must avoid making quota checks a dominant cost for metadata-heavy workloads.","OPERATIONS\n\nUse warning thresholds, hard limits and exception processes. Capacity management should identify growth before users hit hard failures.","LAB\n\nDesign quotas for five teams with different storage allocations and growth rates. Include an escalation path for research bursts.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you prevent one training job from filling shared scratch space and taking down unrelated jobs?", "GOAL\n\nConnect technical controls to cluster governance."),
Day(130,"Storage namespaces","Organizing data for humans and automation","Namespace design affects security, lifecycle, quotas, backup and performance. Good paths communicate ownership and intended use.","EXAMPLE\n\n/home, /projects, /scratch, /datasets, /checkpoints, /archive. Each should have explicit semantics, retention and performance expectations.","AI\n\nKeep temporary high-bandwidth scratch separate from durable datasets. Avoid mixing millions of tiny files with large streaming data without understanding the metadata impact.","OPERATIONS\n\nDocument ownership, lifecycle, cleanup policy, quota and backup class for each namespace.","LAB\n\nCreate a namespace standard for a 256-GPU cluster. Include user, project, dataset, scratch and checkpoint areas.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should ‘scratch’ usually not be treated as a backup repository? Discuss lifecycle and performance intent.","GOAL\n\nDesign namespaces as contracts."),
Day(131,"Storage API design","Automation against infrastructure","Modern storage administration increasingly uses APIs. Understand authentication, idempotency, pagination, rate limits, asynchronous jobs and structured error handling.","API FLOW\n\nAuthenticate → GET state → compare desired state → POST/PATCH change → poll job → verify state → record result.","COMMON FAILURE\n\nA successful HTTP request does not always mean the storage operation completed. Many platforms return asynchronous job IDs.","AUTOMATION\n\nUse retries only for safe operations. Respect status codes, request IDs and server-side rate limits.","LAB\n\nWrite a mock API client that creates a resource, polls an asynchronous job and verifies final state. Add timeout and retry logic.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you make an automation idempotent? Explain desired state, unique identifiers and post-change verification.","GOAL\n\nOperate infrastructure through APIs as confidently as through CLI."),
Day(132,"Storage performance counters","Reading the evidence","Performance engineering requires knowing which counter belongs to which layer.","COUNTER MAP\n\nApplication wait → filesystem latency → block latency → device latency → network RTT/congestion → backend media/controller latency.\n\nNever compare numbers from different layers without context.","PERCENTILES\n\nAverage latency can hide tail latency. p95/p99/p99.9 can reveal pauses that users experience even when average looks healthy.","AI\n\nTraining can be sensitive to synchronized stalls: a few slow ranks can hold an entire collective operation or input pipeline.","LAB\n\nCreate a dashboard vocabulary: IOPS, MB/s, p50/p95/p99 latency, queue depth, CPU, network utilization/errors and metadata ops.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is p99 often more useful than average for distributed AI systems? Explain tail amplification and synchronization.","GOAL\n\nRead telemetry as a causal chain."),
Day(133,"Performance methodology","Scientific benchmarking","Benchmarking is an experiment. Control variables, establish a baseline, change one dimension and record the result.","EXPERIMENT\n\nQuestion → hypothesis → workload → baseline → controlled change → measurement → conclusion → repeatability.","VARIABLES\n\nBlock size, transfer size, queue depth, jobs, threads, clients, read/write mix, dataset size, cache state, filesystem layout and network path.","BAD TEST\n\n‘Storage is 20 GB/s on my server.’ Without workload, client count, cache state and tool configuration, the statement has little architectural value.","LAB\n\nDesign three FIO experiments: latency, random IOPS and sequential throughput. Define exact parameters and success criteria before running them.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you prove a new storage platform is faster than the old one? Discuss matched workloads and statistically meaningful measurements.","GOAL\n\nBecome a performance scientist, not a benchmark screenshot collector."),
Day(134,"Enterprise storage to HPC translation","Use your existing experience correctly","Enterprise storage knowledge is valuable in HPC when you translate concepts: multipathing → redundant fabric, RAID → distributed protection, NFS → user storage, arrays → data platforms, monitoring → observability.","TRANSLATION\n\nTraditional: array + LUN + SAN.\nHPC: distributed storage nodes + parallel filesystem + RDMA fabric + many clients.","WHAT CHANGES\n\nConcurrency rises dramatically. Metadata and data paths separate. Network becomes part of storage performance. Application behavior becomes a first-class storage input.","WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE\n\nFailure domains, capacity planning, change management, monitoring, incident response, performance baselines, documentation and customer communication remain fundamental.","LAB\n\nTake one enterprise storage incident you know well and rewrite it as an HPC incident. Identify which principles transfer and which technologies change.","INTERVIEW\n\n‘You have 12 years of enterprise storage; why HPC?’ Answer: strong operational foundation plus deliberate expansion into parallel storage, RDMA, performance and AI workloads.","GOAL\n\nTurn your previous career into an advantage rather than pretending you are starting from zero.")
)
